{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BQ66PFtDKR8MTHKg2JJyVa","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t- An explicit disable writes at most one `learning_rail_disabled` event per\n101\t  session and switch.\n102\t\n103\t### Changed — `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`: two closed enums gain values\n104\t\n105\t- `learning_rail_disabled.rail` gains `bash_indirect`, and `.switch` gains\n106\t  the two switch names above. No event and no field is added. A consumer\n107\t  that pins the old enum lists rejects the new values.\n108\t- The row's prose still calls ADR-201 `PROPOSED`; the ADR is `ACCEPTED`.\n109\t\n110\t### Changed — hooks and scripts split into modules (PLAN-197, ADR-202)\n111\t\n112\t- Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts. Their\n113\t  implementations move into packages under `.claude/hooks/_lib/`. For\n114\t  example, `_lib/audit_emit.py` goes from 14,522 lines to 496, and\n115\t  `check_bash_safety.py` becomes a 42-line entry point. Among the scripts,\n116\t  `audit-query.py`, `check_contamination.py` and\n117\t  `check-installer-write-safety.py` move the same way.\n118\t- Installs and upgrades receive the packages: the installer copies every\n119\t  `_lib` entry except the test harness. On the merge of pull request #7,\n120\t  the Smoke Install push run passed; a manually dispatched run was\n121\t  cancelled.\n122\t- If you patched a hook by hand, the entry point no longer holds the logic.\n123\t  Re-apply the patch to the module.\n124\t- The compatibility evidence is the test suites. No differential replay\n125\t  against the code before the split is claimed.\n126\t\n127\t### Changed — a registered hook leaves its spool work pending at exit (ADR-055-AMEND-4, partial)\n128\t\n129\t- `_python-hook.sh` exports `CEO_HOOK_BOUNDED_EXIT=1`. A hook that runs\n130\t  with it does not drain its audit spool at exit or on a signal. It leaves\n131\t  an `exit_deadline_skip` breadcrumb instead, and its events wait in the\n132\t  spool for a later drain, so they can reach the log later.\n133\t- This removes the forced exit drain that could outlast a hook's timeout.\n134\t- ADR-055-AMEND-4 is `ACCEPTED` with its implementation `PARTIAL`\n135\t  (Known-open).\n136\t\n137\t### Security and governance — what changed without the signing ceremony\n138\t\n139\t- **How it arrived.** Pull requests #6 and #7 brought 233 canonical paths\n140\t  into `main` with no sentinel and no signature file. Fifteen of them are\n141\t  kernel paths by the kernel list as merged: among them the Bash-guard,\n142\t  canonical-edit, pair-rail, policy and audit-writer hooks, the\n143\t  agent-frontmatter VETO validator, the Codex pin file, the framework's own\n144\t  settings and `validate.yml`. By the list this release restores (Fixed,\n145\t  below), 101 of them are. The merge commit of #7 carries a GPG signature,\n146\t  which is not the signing ceremony. No signed sentinel ratifies those\n147\t  files as of this entry, and nothing in the repository requires one:\n148\t  `main` has no branch protection and no ruleset (measured through the\n149\t  GitHub API on 2026-11-03).\n150\t- **The VETO floor moved from a closed list to family patterns.**\n151\t  - Before, an agent file with `veto_floor: true` had to declare one of\n152\t    four Owner-signed Claude ids.\n153\t  - Now `agent_frontmatter.py` and `escalation_signals.py` also accept any\n154\t    id that matches the families in `_lib/model_currency.py`: Claude Opus\n155\t    majors 4 to 9, `gpt-5` to `gpt-9` and `o3` to `o9`, with numeric and\n156\t    lowercase-word suffixes.\n157\t  - The predicate refuses only the alias `claude-opus-4-1`. It admits ids\n158\t    that the deprecation ledger (`.claude/scripts/model-deprecations.json`)\n159\t    lists as retired or retiring, among them dated Claude Opus 4 and 4.1\n160\t    ids, `gpt-5`, `gpt-5-mini`, `o3`, `o3-mini` and `o4-mini` (probed on\n161\t    2026-11-03).\n162\t  - The docstring of `agent_frontmatter.py` still describes the closed\n163\t    list.\n164\t- **The model allowlist moved to wider family patterns.**\n165\t  - Before, the settings-tamper detector, the agent `model:` check of\n166\t    `validate-governance.sh` and the tier-policy loader accepted only an\n167\t    exact member of a closed list.\n168\t  - Now all three call `is_allowed_model` in `_lib/model_currency.py`\n169\t    (ADR-149 Amendment 4). Besides the listed ids, it admits Claude Opus,\n170\t    Sonnet and Haiku majors 4 to 9, `gpt-5` to `gpt-9`, `o3` to `o9`,\n171\t    `gemini-2` to `gemini-9` and `grok-3` to `grok-9`, with numeric and\n172\t    lowercase-word suffixes.\n173\t  - It refuses only the alias `claude-opus-4-1`, so ids that the\n174\t    deprecation ledger lists as retired pass, among them\n175\t    `claude-sonnet-4-[REDACTED:rg]` and `claude-opus-4-1-[REDACTED:rg]` (probed on\n176\t    2026-11-03).\n177\t  - So `settings_tamper_model_remap`, which `/ceo-boot` reports, no longer\n178\t    flags a model variable (`ANTHROPIC_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL`\n179\t    or an `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_*` key) set to such an id, for example\n180\t    `gemini-2.5-pro` or `grok-4`, in the environment or in a settings\n181\t    `env` block. An agent file with such a `model:` passes\n182\t    `validate-governance.sh`.\n183\t  - The shipped `availableModels` is unchanged.\n184\t- **The Codex pin moved from an exact payload hash to a version floor.**\n185\t  - Before, `check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin` failed closed on any\n186\t    Codex binary whose payload sha256 differed from the signed manifest,\n187\t    which pins one binary of Codex CLI 0.160.0.\n188\t  - Now a Codex CLI newer than the manifest version passes as\n189\t    `compatible/version_floor_only` when a nearby `@openai/codex`\n190\t    `package.json` declares a stable version at or above 0.160.0\n191\t    (`_lib/cli_currency.py`). The payload's sha256 is still computed and\n192\t    recorded, but on this route a mismatch with the manifest no longer\n193\t    fails.\n194\t  - The release gate follows: `validate-pair-rail-verdict.py` only checks\n195\t    the format of the payload sha of a verdict that declares such a\n196\t    version. Its range check reads the pin file; its payload-hash\n197\t    exemption uses the code constant 0.160.0\n198\t    (`cli_currency.CODEX_VERSION_FLOOR`).\n199\t  - The ADR-182 amendment states the floor as `>=0.160.0`. The pin file\n200\t    reads `>=0.128.0`, with no upper bound: a CI fix in the same pull\n201\t    request lowered it after the amendment.\n202\t  - The operating contract still says the reviewer is pinned by hash, not\n203\t    by range. That no longer describes `main`.\n204\t  - Installs do not receive `.claude/governance/`, so there the pin still\n205\t    resolves as infrastructure and fails open (advisory). That is\n206\t    unchanged.\n207\t  - With `--harness codex`, the installer now prints that it was verified\n208\t    against Codex CLI 0.160.0, while the hook fixtures date from 0.139.0.\n209\t\n210\t### Fixed — the kernel's hard-deny list covers the code the v1.4.4 split moved out of seven kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the live-adapter package\n211\t\n212\t- The split (PLAN-197) moved the code of seven kernel entry points into\n213\t  helper packages: five hooks (canonical edit, pair rail, agent spawn,\n214\t  audit log and Bash safety) and two hook library modules (policy and\n215\t  audit emit). The same train replaced the plan-path regexes of two\n216\t  kernel files, the plan-lifecycle hook (`check_plan_edit.py`) and the\n217\t  policy preprocessors (`_lib/policy_preprocessors.py`), with the\n218\t  plan-path predicate in `_lib/plan_paths.py`. The kernel's hard-deny\n219\t  list (`check_arbitration_kernel.py`) kept naming only the kernel files,\n220\t  so the 81 files of those packages and the predicate were canonical\n221\t  only: a signed sentinel could unlock them. The list now has a row for\n222\t  each, so that code is hard-deny again, as it was inside the kernel\n223\t  files in v1.4.2.\n224\t- The list also enrolls four modules that kernel code calls, which were\n225\t  canonical only as well: `_lib/model_currency.py`, the VETO-floor\n226\t  predicate that the kernel files `agent_frontmatter.py` and\n227\t  `escalation_signals.py` now call as part of their VETO-floor check; and\n228\t  three modules this train added, `_lib/cli_currency.py` (the Codex\n229\t  payload-pin currency check, called by the pair-rail code),"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dc5b5fe9-2c6b-46ea-80f3-b2c4ce2f3240","uuid":"ecb44dfb-905f-4138-a927-78860585cd97","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:27:55.803Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/CHANGELOG.md","content":"- An explicit disable writes at most one `learning_rail_disabled` event per\n  session and switch.\n\n### Changed — `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`: two closed enums gain values\n\n- `learning_rail_disabled.rail` gains `bash_indirect`, and `.switch` gains\n  the two switch names above. No event and no field is added. A consumer\n  that pins the old enum lists rejects the new values.\n- The row's prose still calls ADR-201 `PROPOSED`; the ADR is `ACCEPTED`.\n\n### Changed — hooks and scripts split into modules (PLAN-197, ADR-202)\n\n- Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts. Their\n  implementations move into packages under `.claude/hooks/_lib/`. For\n  example, `_lib/audit_emit.py` goes from 14,522 lines to 496, and\n  `check_bash_safety.py` becomes a 42-line entry point. Among the scripts,\n  `audit-query.py`, `check_contamination.py` and\n  `check-installer-write-safety.py` move the same way.\n- Installs and upgrades receive the packages: the installer copies every\n  `_lib` entry except the test harness. On the merge of pull request #7,\n  the Smoke Install push run passed; a manually dispatched run was\n  cancelled.\n- If you patched a hook by hand, the entry point no longer holds the logic.\n  Re-apply the patch to the module.\n- The compatibility evidence is the test suites. No differential replay\n  against the code before the split is claimed.\n\n### Changed — a registered hook leaves its spool work pending at exit (ADR-055-AMEND-4, partial)\n\n- `_python-hook.sh` exports `CEO_HOOK_BOUNDED_EXIT=1`. A hook that runs\n  with it does not drain its audit spool at exit or on a signal. It leaves\n  an `exit_deadline_skip` breadcrumb instead, and its events wait in the\n  spool for a later drain, so they can reach the log later.\n- This removes the forced exit drain that could outlast a hook's timeout.\n- ADR-055-AMEND-4 is `ACCEPTED` with its implementation `PARTIAL`\n  (Known-open).\n\n### Security and governance — what changed without the signing ceremony\n\n- **How it arrived.** Pull requests #6 and #7 brought 233 canonical paths\n  into `main` with no sentinel and no signature file. Fifteen of them are\n  kernel paths by the kernel list as merged: among them the Bash-guard,\n  canonical-edit, pair-rail, policy and audit-writer hooks, the\n  agent-frontmatter VETO validator, the Codex pin file, the framework's own\n  settings and `validate.yml`. By the list this release restores (Fixed,\n  below), 101 of them are. The merge commit of #7 carries a GPG signature,\n  which is not the signing ceremony. No signed sentinel ratifies those\n  files as of this entry, and nothing in the repository requires one:\n  `main` has no branch protection and no ruleset (measured through the\n  GitHub API on 2026-11-03).\n- **The VETO floor moved from a closed list to family patterns.**\n  - Before, an agent file with `veto_floor: true` had to declare one of\n    four Owner-signed Claude ids.\n  - Now `agent_frontmatter.py` and `escalation_signals.py` also accept any\n    id that matches the families in `_lib/model_currency.py`: Claude Opus\n    majors 4 to 9, `gpt-5` to `gpt-9` and `o3` to `o9`, with numeric and\n    lowercase-word suffixes.\n  - The predicate refuses only the alias `claude-opus-4-1`. It admits ids\n    that the deprecation ledger (`.claude/scripts/model-deprecations.json`)\n    lists as retired or retiring, among them dated Claude Opus 4 and 4.1\n    ids, `gpt-5`, `gpt-5-mini`, `o3`, `o3-mini` and `o4-mini` (probed on\n    2026-11-03).\n  - The docstring of `agent_frontmatter.py` still describes the closed\n    list.\n- **The model allowlist moved to wider family patterns.**\n  - Before, the settings-tamper detector, the agent `model:` check of\n    `validate-governance.sh` and the tier-policy loader accepted only an\n    exact member of a closed list.\n  - Now all three call `is_allowed_model` in `_lib/model_currency.py`\n    (ADR-149 Amendment 4). Besides the listed ids, it admits Claude Opus,\n    Sonnet and Haiku majors 4 to 9, `gpt-5` to `gpt-9`, `o3` to `o9`,\n    `gemini-2` to `gemini-9` and `grok-3` to `grok-9`, with numeric and\n    lowercase-word suffixes.\n  - It refuses only the alias `claude-opus-4-1`, so ids that the\n    deprecation ledger lists as retired pass, among them\n    `claude-sonnet-4-[REDACTED:rg]` and `claude-opus-4-1-[REDACTED:rg]` (probed on\n    2026-11-03).\n  - So `settings_tamper_model_remap`, which `/ceo-boot` reports, no longer\n    flags a model variable (`ANTHROPIC_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL`\n    or an `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_*` key) set to such an id, for example\n    `gemini-2.5-pro` or `grok-4`, in the environment or in a settings\n    `env` block. An agent file with such a `model:` passes\n    `validate-governance.sh`.\n  - The shipped `availableModels` is unchanged.\n- **The Codex pin moved from an exact payload hash to a version floor.**\n  - Before, `check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin` failed closed on any\n    Codex binary whose payload sha256 differed from the signed manifest,\n    which pins one binary of Codex CLI 0.160.0.\n  - Now a Codex CLI newer than the manifest version passes as\n    `compatible/version_floor_only` when a nearby `@openai/codex`\n    `package.json` declares a stable version at or above 0.160.0\n    (`_lib/cli_currency.py`). The payload's sha256 is still computed and\n    recorded, but on this route a mismatch with the manifest no longer\n    fails.\n  - The release gate follows: `validate-pair-rail-verdict.py` only checks\n    the format of the payload sha of a verdict that declares such a\n    version. Its range check reads the pin file; its payload-hash\n    exemption uses the code constant 0.160.0\n    (`cli_currency.CODEX_VERSION_FLOOR`).\n  - The ADR-182 amendment states the floor as `>=0.160.0`. The pin file\n    reads `>=0.128.0`, with no upper bound: a CI fix in the same pull\n    request lowered it after the amendment.\n  - The operating contract still says the reviewer is pinned by hash, not\n    by range. That no longer describes `main`.\n  - Installs do not receive `.claude/governance/`, so there the pin still\n    resolves as infrastructure and fails open (advisory). That is\n    unchanged.\n  - With `--harness codex`, the installer now prints that it was verified\n    against Codex CLI 0.160.0, while the hook fixtures date from 0.139.0.\n\n### Fixed — the kernel's hard-deny list covers the code the v1.4.4 split moved out of seven kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the live-adapter package\n\n- The split (PLAN-197) moved the code of seven kernel entry points into\n  helper packages: five hooks (canonical edit, pair rail, agent spawn,\n  audit log and Bash safety) and two hook library modules (policy and\n  audit emit). The same train replaced the plan-path regexes of two\n  kernel files, the plan-lifecycle hook (`check_plan_edit.py`) and the\n  policy preprocessors (`_lib/policy_preprocessors.py`), with the\n  plan-path predicate in `_lib/plan_paths.py`. The kernel's hard-deny\n  list (`check_arbitration_kernel.py`) kept naming only the kernel files,\n  so the 81 files of those packages and the predicate were canonical\n  only: a signed sentinel could unlock them. The list now has a row for\n  each, so that code is hard-deny again, as it was inside the kernel\n  files in v1.4.2.\n- The list also enrolls four modules that kernel code calls, which were\n  canonical only as well: `_lib/model_currency.py`, the VETO-floor\n  predicate that the kernel files `agent_frontmatter.py` and\n  `escalation_signals.py` now call as part of their VETO-floor check; and\n  three modules this train added, `_lib/cli_currency.py` (the Codex\n  payload-pin currency check, called by the pair-rail code),","numLines":130,"startLine":100,"totalLines":2658}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BQ66PFtDKR8MTHKg2JJyVa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
